/**
 * \page build_system Build System
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * The build is organized so that *decisions* (what to build, with which options) live at
 * the root, *capabilities* (reusable flags, defines, helper functions) live under `cmake/`,
 * and each library only *declares what it is and what it depends on*. Adding a subsystem is
 * one `worse_add_library()` block plus a one-line dependency edge — not edits scattered
 * across a monolithic script.
 *
 * \section bs_layout Repository layout
 *
 * \code{.unparsed}
 * CMakeLists.txt              # thin entry: project() + global C++ settings, then include + add_subdirectory
 * cmake/
 *   WorseOptions.cmake        # every option() in one place
 *   WorsePlatform.cmake       # host platform/arch -> WE_* preprocessor defines
 *   WorseCompileOptions.cmake # worse::build_options (PUBLIC) and worse::warnings (PRIVATE)
 *   WorseHelpers.cmake        # worse_add_library / worse_add_test / worse_add_bench
 *   WorseDocs.cmake           # the Doxygen `docs` target
 * src/worse/
 *   CMakeLists.txt            # `worse` facade target (aggregates the enabled subsystems)
 *   core/CMakeLists.txt       # worse::core subsystem library
 * tests/{,core/}CMakeLists.txt   # one test executable per sub-area
 * bench/{,core/}CMakeLists.txt   # the bench_core binary
 * \endcode
 *
 * The root `CMakeLists.txt` does nothing but `project()`, a handful of global settings, and
 * `include()` of the four capability modules in order (options → platform → compile options →
 * helpers), then `add_subdirectory()` for `src/worse`, `tests`, and `bench`.
 *
 * \section bs_presets Presets
 *
 * All builds go through `CMakePresets.json`, which wires the vcpkg toolchain from
 * `$VCPKG_ROOT` and the Ninja generator. Output lands in `build/<preset>/`.
 *
 * | Preset | Build type | Adds |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | `debug`          | Debug          | assertions on (default day-to-day) |
 * | `release`        | Release        | optimized, `NDEBUG` |
 * | `relwithdebinfo` | RelWithDebInfo | optimized + line info |
 * | `asan`           | RelWithDebInfo | ASan + UBSan, assertions forced on (see \ref bs_sanitizers) |
 * | `lto`            | Release        | ThinLTO / IPO |
 *
 * \code{.sh}
 * cmake --preset debug                 # configure
 * cmake --build  build/debug           # build everything (libs + tests)
 * ctest --preset debug                 # run the full suite
 *
 * ctest --preset debug -R '^core_math\.'   # run just one sub-area (see bs_tests)
 * ctest --preset asan                      # full suite under AddressSanitizer
 * \endcode
 *
 * \section bs_options Options
 *
 * Set with `-DWORSE_…=ON` at configure time, or bake into a preset's `cacheVariables`.
 *
 * | Option | Default | Effect |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_TESTS`       | ON when top-level | build the unit tests |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_BENCH`       | OFF | build the benchmarks (needs the vcpkg `bench` feature) |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_DOCS`        | OFF | expose the Doxygen `docs` target |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_SHARED`      | OFF | build the engine libraries as shared instead of static |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_CORE`        | ON  | build the `core` subsystem |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_TESTS_CORE`  | ON  | build the core tests |
 * | `WORSE_BUILD_BENCH_CORE`  | ON  | build the core benches |
 * | `WORSE_ENABLE_WERROR`     | OFF | warnings as errors |
 * | `WORSE_ENABLE_LTO`        | OFF | IPO/LTO for the optimized configs |
 * | `WORSE_ENABLE_SANITIZERS` | OFF | ASan + UBSan |
 * | `WORSE_ENABLE_ASSERTS`    | OFF | force `WE_ASSERT` on even in `NDEBUG` builds |
 * | `WORSE_FORCE_SCALAR_SIMD` | OFF | disable SSE/NEON, use the scalar SIMD fallback |
 *
 * \section bs_naming Naming convention
 *
 * One rule disambiguates the two namespaces of identifiers:
 *
 * - **`WORSE_*`** — build-system identifiers: CMake variables, `option()`s, the `worse_*`
 *   target prefix.
 * - **`WE_*`** — C++ preprocessor symbols visible to code: macros and `#if defined` switches.
 *
 * The build injects this set of `WE_*` symbols (carried PUBLICly by `worse::build_options`, so
 * they reach every TU — macros do **not** propagate through `import`):
 *
 * | Symbol | Source |
 * |---|---|
 * | `WE_PLATFORM_WINDOWS` / `_MACOS` / `_LINUX` | `CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME` |
 * | `WE_ARCH_AMD64` / `WE_ARCH_AARCH64`         | `CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR` |
 * | `WE_BUILD_DEBUG` / `WE_BUILD_RELEASE`       | the build config |
 * | `WE_VERSION_MAJOR` / `_MINOR` / `_PATCH`    | `project(VERSION)` |
 * | `WE_ENABLE_ASSERTS`                         | `WORSE_ENABLE_ASSERTS` |
 * | `WE_SIMD_FORCE_SCALAR`                      | `WORSE_FORCE_SCALAR_SIMD` |
 *
 * \section bs_libs Libraries, compile options, and dependencies
 *
 * Every engine library is created through `worse_add_library(<subsystem> …)`, which produces a
 * `worse_<subsystem>` target plus a `worse::<subsystem>` alias and, by default, links two
 * interface carriers:
 *
 * - **`worse::build_options`** (PUBLIC) — propagating: the C++ standard level, the `WE_*`
 *   defines above, and sanitizer/LTO flags. PUBLIC so consumers inherit the same defines.
 * - **`worse::warnings`** (PRIVATE) — non-propagating: `/W4 /permissive-` (MSVC) or
 *   `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic` (Clang/GCC), plus `-Werror` when `WORSE_ENABLE_WERROR`.
 *
 * Dependencies between subsystems are declared as edges, not implied by directory order:
 *
 * \code{.cmake}
 * worse_add_library(core
 *     MODULES ${CORE_MODULES}    # C++20 interface units, listed explicitly (the BMI graph)
 *     HEADERS ${CORE_HEADERS}    # textual headers, globbed
 *     SOURCES ${CORE_SOURCES})   # implementation TUs, globbed
 *     # core is the base — no PUBLIC_DEPS
 * \endcode
 *
 * Module interface units (`.cppm`) are listed explicitly because they define the public module
 * graph and the BMI build order, so additions should be intentional and reviewable; `.cpp`/`.hpp`
 * are globbed per directory with `CONFIGURE_DEPENDS`. A subsystem's modules are PUBLIC in a
 * `FILE_SET CXX_MODULES`, so any target that links it can `import` them (CMake ≥ 3.28).
 *
 * \note Library granularity is **one subsystem per directory under `src/worse/`**. `core` is a
 *       single library; container/algorithm/math/coroutine are module partitions *inside* it
 *       (container and algorithm are mutually dependent via `iterator`/`sort`, so they cannot be
 *       separate targets). Future subsystems — e.g. `RHI`, `render` — are sibling libraries.
 *
 * \section bs_add_subsystem Adding a subsystem
 *
 * To add, say, an `RHI` library that builds on `core`:
 *
 * **1. Declare the library** in `src/worse/RHI/CMakeLists.txt`:
 * \code{.cmake}
 * worse_add_library(rhi
 *     MODULES rhi.cppm device.cppm swapchain.cppm
 *     PUBLIC_DEPS worse::core)        # the dependency edge — one line
 * \endcode
 *
 * **2. Wire it in** from `src/worse/CMakeLists.txt` — gate it, then aggregate into the facade:
 * \code{.cmake}
 * if(WORSE_BUILD_RHI)
 *     add_subdirectory(RHI)
 * endif()
 * if(TARGET worse::rhi)
 *     target_link_libraries(worse PUBLIC worse::rhi)
 * endif()
 * \endcode
 *
 * **3. Register the options** `WORSE_BUILD_RHI` (and optional `WORSE_BUILD_TESTS_RHI` /
 * `_BENCH_RHI`) in `cmake/WorseOptions.cmake`, and mirror the directory under `tests/` and
 * `bench/`.
 *
 * Per-library symbols or flags beyond the shared carriers go on the same call via the
 * `DEFINES` (PUBLIC) and `OPTIONS` (PRIVATE) arguments of `worse_add_library`.
 *
 * \section bs_tests Tests and benchmarks
 *
 * Tests are split per sub-area into independent executables —
 * `test_core_container`, `test_core_algorithm`, `test_core_math`, `test_core` — via
 * `worse_add_test()`. Editing one suite only relinks that executable, and each registers with
 * a ctest prefix so groups select cleanly:
 *
 * \code{.sh}
 * cmake --build build/debug --target test_core_math   # build just the math suite
 * ctest --preset debug -R '^core_container\.'          # run just the container group
 * \endcode
 *
 * Benchmarks are intentionally a **single `bench_core` binary** (not split): `bench_main.cpp`
 * drives every suite against one shared `nanobench::Bench` so the relative-speed column compares
 * across the whole run, and it is the sole owner of the nanobench implementation and EASTL's
 * allocator hooks. Benches build `-O2 -DNDEBUG` so the template-heavy engine code is optimized
 * and `WE_ASSERT` bounds checks compile out. nanobench + EASTL come from the vcpkg `bench`
 * manifest feature:
 *
 * \code{.sh}
 * cmake --preset release -DWORSE_BUILD_BENCH=ON -DVCPKG_MANIFEST_FEATURES=bench
 * cmake --build build/release --target bench_core
 * ./build/release/bench/core/bench_core
 * \endcode
 *
 * \section bs_sanitizers Sanitizers, LTO, and assertions
 *
 * `WORSE_ENABLE_SANITIZERS` adds matching ASan + UBSan compile and link flags;
 * `WORSE_ENABLE_LTO` enables ThinLTO/IPO for the optimized configs after probing support.
 *
 * \warning The `asan` preset is **RelWithDebInfo, not Debug**, on purpose: on Windows the clang
 * AddressSanitizer is incompatible with the debug CRT (`/MDd` → spurious *bad-free* inside
 * `ucrtbased`). RelWithDebInfo keeps the release CRT (`/MD`) plus line info. On Windows the ASan
 * runtime is a DLL with no static variant, so the build stages
 * `clang_rt.asan_dynamic-x86_64.dll` next to each executable — otherwise it dies at startup with
 * `0xc0000135` (including during gtest's build-time test discovery).
 *
 * Assertions are decoupled from the build type: `WE_ASSERT` / `WE_ASSERT_MSG` are active unless
 * `NDEBUG` is defined **and** `WE_ENABLE_ASSERTS` is not, so `WORSE_ENABLE_ASSERTS=ON` keeps them
 * live in optimized builds (the `asan` preset turns this on). `WE_VERIFY` is always on regardless.
 */
